$DIR/main.p:19:11: p holds a view of s (&s.c.x, line 17), which was changed by t.c = ..., which drops the old value of t.c at line 18, so p can no longer be used here
    p looks at memory that s refers to, without owning it: the view was made by
    &s.c.x at line 17. A view obtained through a counted reference held in a
    local variable may be used only while the variable keeps the reference the
    view was made from. At line 18 s was changed by t.c = ..., which drops the
    old value of t.c, so the view may look at memory that was released, or at a
    value that now belongs to something else.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use p before line 18, or give s its new value only after the last use of p
    Or make the view again after line 18:
        p = &s.c.x
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)
